From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jens Axboe <axboe@kernel.dk>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:19:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] arm64: dts: apple: t6050: keep the PMP's power domains
 on

The PMP's firmware runs from the moment its driver starts it, and in a
suspend-to-idle the driver leaves it running: only a firmware-driven sleep
stops it. Its three power states were ordinary genpd domains, though, so
at the noirq phase of every s2idle genpd gated the PMP's core (pmp) and
its SRAM (pms-sram) under the running firmware and then tried their parent
(pms), which the PMGR refuses:

  PS pms: Failed to reach power state 0x0 (requested 0xf0000f0, now: 0x1f0002ff)

With power back at the wake the firmware usually takes a translation fault
within the first moments (PC 0x10178b4, 0x1025498 or 0, every register
zero) and reports a crash; it stays down until reboot, and prints its
crash log again at each later wake. On the K116s that was 3 of the 4
suspends that reached the noirq phase with a live PMP.

Mark all three always-on, as the ADT's "critical" flag on PMP asks.
Every other SoC description with a pms domain keeps it on, and those
whose PMP Linux runs (T6030, T604x, T8122, T8132, T8140 and the T8142
J813, which gained it for the same crash) keep pmp on. The PMP then
keeps running through s2idle as it does under macOS, and the pms
message goes away with the power-off request.

Signed-off-by: Jens Axboe <axboe@kernel.dk>
[thomas: name the SoCs that keep pms and pmp on]
Signed-off-by: Thomas Glanzmann <thomas@glanzmann.de>

(cherry picked from commit f61eb4550a95208ea2acd3a5bc614944735a4ff9)
[k114s: from aurora-silicon/linux pull request 1789 (open), unchanged]
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-peripherals.dtsi | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-peripherals.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-peripherals.dtsi
index 2f4da05c2..6d2bd0047 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-peripherals.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/apple/t6050-peripherals.dtsi
@@ -94,6 +94,12 @@ ps_pms: power-controller@100 {
 		#power-domain-cells = <0>;
 		#reset-cells = <0>;
 		label = "pms";
+		/*
+		 * The PMP's firmware runs from boot until a real sleep stops it, on its own
+		 * core (pmp, "critical" in the ADT) and out of its own SRAM (pms-sram): none
+		 * of the three may be gated under it, s2idle included.
+		 */
+		apple,always-on;
 		status = "disabled";
 	};
 	ps_pmp: power-controller@128 {
@@ -103,6 +109,7 @@ ps_pmp: power-controller@128 {
 		#reset-cells = <0>;
 		label = "pmp";
 		power-domains = <&ps_pms>;
+		apple,always-on;
 		status = "disabled";
 	};
 	ps_pms_sram: power-controller@130 {
@@ -112,6 +119,7 @@ ps_pms_sram: power-controller@130 {
 		#reset-cells = <0>;
 		label = "pms-sram";
 		power-domains = <&ps_pms>;
+		apple,always-on;
 		status = "disabled";
 	};
 	ps_fab0_piogw: power-controller@1d8 {
